    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the city-capacity rule for `POST /api/owners`: creating an owner is now rejected with **409** when the owner's city already contains 50 or more owners.\n\nFollowing the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions, I added:\n\n1. **`EnsureCityCapacity.java`** (`function/owner/`) \u2014 a pipeline step that reads the request DTO via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, counts existing owners in the same city (case-insensitively, matching the `AssignCustomerCode` comparison style) using `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, and throws when the count is \u2265 50. It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the owner being created isn't counted.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** (`escalation/`) \u2014 a checked exception carrying the city and count.\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** (`escalation/`) \u2014 responds 409 with a `ProblemDetail` body, mirroring the existing duplicate handlers.\n\n4. **`...CityAtCapacityException.yml`** (`officefloor/escalation/`) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `ensureCityCapacity` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `ensureUniqueHousehold` and `build`.\n\nThe new acceptance test `coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fills a fresh city to exactly 50, then expects the 51st to be a 409) passes, and no existing tests regressed.",
